  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWeak west to northwest flow aloft transitions to more southwest \nthrough the day Monday, ahead of our next weather maker digging\nacross the West Coast. Dry weather remains on tap for Monday, \nwith patchy fog and stratus diminishing through the morning across\nthe plains, as some high level waa clouds spreads in from west to \neast across the area through the afternoon. High temperatures also \nwarm slightly into the 50s across the plains and mainly 40s and 50s \nacross the higher terrain. The slow increase in southwest flow helps \nto develop lee troughing across the plains Monday night, with lows \nin the 20s and 30s once again across the region, warmest in the \nbanana belt, with developing westerly flow will keep temperatures up \novernight. \n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, latest operational models and ensemble\ndata are in good agreement of increasing southwest flow aloft through\nthe day Tuesday, as the West Coast upper trough translates across\nthe Intermountain West and Great Basin. This will start to bring \nsome moisture into the Continental Divide with increasing chances\nof precipitation through the day. Moderate west to southwest flow \nacross the region Tuesday night into Wednesday as said broad trough\ncontinues to translate east across the Rockies and into the high \nplains, will keep the best precipitation coverage across the higher\nterrain, mainly along and west of the Continental Divide Tuesday\nnight, with precipitation diminishing behind the passing system\nthrough the day Wednesday. The quick movement of the system will\nlimit precipitation totals, with the potential for low end advisory\nsnow amounts possible, mainly across the eastern San Juan mountains. \n\nThe increased downslope and mixing will help boost temperatures back \nabove seasonal levels in the 60s across the plains both Tuesday and\nWednesday, with the potential for stronger westerly winds in the lee\nof the eastern mountains Tuesday night into early Wednesday morning.   \n\nFor Thursday through Sunday, operational models and ensemble mean\ndata continue to trend further north and not as deep with the next \neastern Pacific system digging across the Great Basin and lifting \nout across the Northern Rockies. This will keep the best chances of \nprecipitation once again along and west of the Continental Divide \nThursday night through Friday night, with moderate to strong westerly \nflow keeping generally warm, dry and breezy conditions in place across \nthe eastern Plains.  Moderate north to northwest flow behind the passing \nsystem to keep slightly cooler and mainly day conditions across the \nregion for Saturday and Sunday.",
